"An event-time window diagram shows how events are assigned to windows and when results may be emitted or revised.\n\nUse when: Explain late events and watermark-dependent results. Compare tumbling, sliding, or session window behavior.\n\nAvoid when: The question is service topology rather than temporal grouping.\n\nVisual grammar: An explicit time axis positions event timestamps and window intervals. Arrival markers distinguish processing time from event time. Watermark and trigger annotations identify completeness estimates and emission rules.\n\nBuild: Declare window type, time units, and timestamp source. Place a small synthetic event set with explicit arrival order. Draw window membership, watermark progress, and allowed lateness. Use separate lanes for events, windows, and emissions so labels cannot collide.\n\nCheck: Boundary events belong to the correct intervals. Overlapping windows intentionally duplicate event membership. A watermark is presented as a progress estimate rather than proof of no later events. Intervals and annotations do not intersect event labels.\n\nPitfalls: Treating arrival order as event-time order. Omitting trigger or lateness rules and implying final results too early.\n\nExample: An event timestamped 10:04 arrives after the 10:05 watermark and follows the declared allowed-lateness rule.\n\nAlternatives: Stream processing topology, Timing diagram."
